    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2031414417850560972",
      "url": "https://x.com/Gianl1974/status/2031414417850560972",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/Gianl1974/status/2031414417850560972",
      "text": "Five members of the Iranian women’s national football team have sought asylum in Australia after refusing to sing the Islamic Republic’s national anthem\n\nThe team was competing on the Gold Coast but lost their group matches: 3-0 to South Korea, 4-0 to hosts Australia, and 2-0 to the Philippines on March 8, ending their tournament run early. Amid the ongoing US-Israel war against Iran (which escalated with strikes starting late February 2026), the players' actions during matches sparked major controversy.\n\nIn their opener against South Korea (March 2 or 3), the entire team stood in silence and did not sing or salute during the Iranian national anthem—a move widely interpreted as a silent protest or show of mourning/defiance against the regime. Iranian state TV and commentators branded them \"wartime traitors\" in the \"pinnacle of dishonor,\" with calls for severe punishment (potentially including detention, execution, or worse, as treason is a capital offense in Iran). \n\nReports indicate players were pressured (via threats to families back home) to sing and salute in later games against Australia and the Philippines.\n\nThis has triggered widespread international alarm for their safety if forced to return home. \n\nEarlier, U.S. President Donald Trump wrote:\nAustralia is making a terrible humanitarian mistake by allowing the Iran National Woman’s Soccer team to be forced back to Iran, where they will most likely be killed. Don’t do it, Mr. Prime Minister, give ASYLUM. The U.S. will take them if you won’t.\n\nAustralian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese now granted the asylum for the women's soccer team.",
      "source": "Twitter for iPhone",
